<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- THE PROMISE: there is nothing here to permit.

         This is the only module in the suite that declares no permission at all, and the absence is
         the feature. Finance asks for INTERNET because it has to phone a bank on your behalf and
         says so at length; Health reaches RxNorm; Citation fetches catalogues. A vault has nobody to
         phone. So:

           1. NO INTERNET. Not disabled, not gated behind a setting: absent. There is no HTTP client
              on this module's classpath, no sync, no account, no telemetry, and no breach-check
              lookup — not even the "k-anonymous" kind that sends the first five characters of a
              password hash to somebody else's server. Whatever is in the vault has no route off the
              device that this module could take.

           2. NO EXPORT. Every component below is `exported="false"`. The Operations Sandbox opens
              this app in the same process; nothing outside the app can start it, and there is no
              content provider, no receiver and no service for anything else to bind to.

           3. NO BACKUP AGENT, NO AUTO-BACKUP OF ITS OWN. The vault travels in exactly one way — the
              sandbox's archive, as the sealed file, through `SecretsBackupContributor`. It is safe
              there because the key that opens it is a passphrase in somebody's head rather than
              anything the archive or the phone contains. The *device* unlock (the fingerprint
              shortcut) is the opposite: Keystore-wrapped, device-bound, and deliberately excluded
              from the archive, because a convenience that survived onto another phone would not be
              a convenience.

           4. FLAG_SECURE, while the vault is open. The activity blocks screenshots and keeps itself
              out of the recents thumbnail — see MainActivity. That is a real leak with a cheap fix:
              the recents screenshot is written to disk by the system, and a vault list is exactly
              the picture nobody wants there. -->

    <!-- Secrets is a library module: no app-level attributes here (name/icon/theme/label live on
         the :app host). Only this app's own components are declared for the manifest merger. -->
    <application>

        <!-- Not a launcher, and not exported: the Operations Sandbox home opens this explicitly,
             in-process. `singleTask` so that returning to it never leaves a second, older copy of
             the unlock screen behind the current one. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:excludeFromRecents="false"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Secrets"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

    </application>

</manifest>
